{"name":"Firefly Glow","hex":"#fff3a1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"firefly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any beetle of the family Lampyridae, which exhibit bioluminescence during twilight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/firefly"},{"term":"glow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To emit heat and light without a flame.","To emit heat and light without a flame."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A state of heat and light being emitted by a hot object.","A state of heat being emitted by a person or an animal's body."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Firefly","blocks":[{"text":"The Lampyridae are a family of elateroid beetles with more than 2,400 described species, many of which are light-emitting. They are soft-bodied beetles commonly called fireflies, lightning bugs, or glowworms for their conspicuous emission of light, mainly during twilight, to attract mates. The type species is Lampyris noctiluca, the common glow-worm of Europe. Light production in the Lampyridae is thought to have originated as a warning signal that the larvae were distasteful. This ability to create light was then co-opted as a mating signal and, in a further development, adult female fireflies of the genus Photuris mimic the flash pattern of the Photinus beetle to trap their males as prey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Photuris_lucicrescens.jpg/330px-Photuris_lucicrescens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Firefly"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif/500px-Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif/500px-Lampyris_Noctiluca_%28firefly%29_mating.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALampyris_Noctiluca_(firefly)_mating.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Luciola4_crop.png/500px-Luciola4_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Luciola4_crop.png/500px-Luciola4_crop.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A larviform female with light-emitting organs on her abdomen. Unlike larvae, she has compound eyes.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALuciola4_crop.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Firefly_composite.jpg/500px-Firefly_composite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Firefly_composite.jpg/500px-Firefly_composite.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Photuris female by flash photography (above); by her own light (below)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFirefly_composite.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Leuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG/500px-Leuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Leuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG/960px-Leuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Leuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG/500px-Leuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALeuchtk%C3%A4fer_-_Firefly.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb